  "key": "constitution",
  "title": "Constitutional Instruments",
  "description": "The foundational instruments of the United Kingdom's uncodified constitution — from Magna Carta to the Parliament Acts.",
  "custodian": "Cabinet Office · Ministry of Justice",
  "polity": "uk",
  "preamble": "# Constitutional Instruments\n\nThe United Kingdom has no single constitutional document. Its constitution is an\naccumulation: charters extracted from kings, settlements imposed after revolutions,\nand statutes that rebalanced the chambers of Parliament itself. This codex gathers the\ninstruments generally recognised as constitutional bedrock.\n\nThese documents span 796 years. Between the sealing of Magna Carta in a meadow at\nRunnymede (1215) and the Parliament Act 1949, the pattern repeats: a crisis, a demand\nfor limits on power, and a text. Each text here is transcribed with its true history —\nwho forced it, who assented, what later Parliaments kept and what they repealed.\n\n## Documents in this codex\n\n| Document | Citation | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Magna Carta (1297 statute) | 25 Edw. 1 cc. 1, 9, 29 | Partially in force |\n| Habeas Corpus Act 1679 | 31 Cha. 2 c. 2 | In force (amended) |\n| Bill of Rights 1689 | 1 Will. & Mar. Sess. 2 c. 2 | In force (amended) |\n| Act of Settlement 1701 | 12 & 13 Will. 3 c. 2 | In force (amended) |\n| Union with Scotland Act 1706 | 6 Ann. c. 11 | In force (amended) |\n| Parliament Act 1911 | 1 & 2 Geo. 5 c. 13 | In force (amended) |\n| Parliament Act 1949 | 12, 13 & 14 Geo. 6 c. 103 | In force |\n\n## Why these live together\n\nEach instrument in this codex answers the same question — *where does power stop?* —\nand each has been amended or invoked by the others. The Parliament Acts modify the\nprocedure by which every other document here could be amended; the Bill of Rights and\nthe Act of Settlement together define the Crown that gives assent. They form one\ndependency graph and therefore one codex.",
