  "title": "Devolution Settlements",
  "description": "The re-distribution of power to Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland — three settlements, three referendums, three legislatures.",
  "custodian": "Cabinet Office · Territorial Offices",
  "polity": "uk",
  "preamble": "# Devolution Settlements\n\nIn 1998 the United Kingdom performed the largest redistribution of legislative power in\nits modern history: three statutes created (or re-created) legislatures in Edinburgh,\nCardiff and Belfast, each authorised by referendum. In this platform's vocabulary,\ndevolution is the **fork**: a polity derives new institutions from the parent codex\nwhile remaining within the Union's statute branch.\n\n## Documents in this codex\n\n| Document | Citation | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Scotland Act 1998 | 1998 c. 46 | In force (amended) |\n| Northern Ireland Act 1998 | 1998 c. 47 | In force (amended) |\n| Government of Wales Act 2006 | 2006 c. 32 | In force (amended) |\n\n## Why these live together\n\nThree parallel settlements, drafted in the same two years, amended in visible dialogue\nwith one another (reserved-powers models, permanence clauses, consent conventions), and\neach carrying a referendum inscription — the people's assent recorded before the\nchambers'. They are the template for how this platform models multi-level polities.",
