  "polity": "uk",
  "preamble": "# Europe & Treaties\n\nForty-eight years of constitutional history in three statutes: the Act that made\nEuropean law flow into the United Kingdom (1972), the Act that cut the conduit (2018),\nand the Act that settled the terms of departure (2020). No other codex shows so\nclearly why history must be preserved rather than overwritten — half a century of law\nwas made under the 1972 Act, and understanding today's \"assimilated law\" requires the\nfull chain of inscriptions.\n\n## Documents in this codex\n\n| Document | Citation | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| European Communities Act 1972 | 1972 c. 68 | Repealed |\n| European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 | 2018 c. 16 | In force (amended) |\n| European Union (Withdrawal Agreement) Act 2020 | 2020 c. 1 | In force |\n\n## Why these live together\n\nThey are one story: accession, incorporation, referendum, withdrawal, and the\nmanagement of the residue. Each Act expressly amends or repeals its predecessor, and\nthe referendum inscriptions of 1975 and 2016 bracket the deepest constitutional\nargument in modern British history.",
