  "preamble": "# Governance & Elections\n\nThis codex holds the machinery: the statutes that decide who may vote, who may sit in\neach House, how the senior judiciary is appointed, and how a Parliament ends. It is the\nmost actively amended codex in the United Kingdom polity — its newest document received\nRoyal Assent in March 2026.\n\n## Documents in this codex\n\n| Document | Citation | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Representation of the People Act 1918 | 7 & 8 Geo. 5 c. 64 | Largely superseded |\n| Representation of the People (Equal Franchise) Act 1928 | 18 & 19 Geo. 5 c. 12 | Superseded (consolidated) |\n| House of Lords Act 1999 | 1999 c. 34 | In force (amended) |\n| Constitutional Reform Act 2005 | 2005 c. 4 | In force |\n| Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 | 2011 c. 14 | Repealed |\n| Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022 | 2022 c. 11 | In force |\n| House of Lords (Hereditary Peers) Act 2026 | 2026 c. 12 | In force |\n\n## Why these live together\n\nFranchise, composition, judiciary, dissolution: four subsystems of one machine. The\ndocuments amend and repeal one another in a visible chain — the 1928 Act completes the\n1918 Act; the 2022 Act repeals the 2011 Act; the 2026 Act finishes what the 1999 Act\nbegan. Reading them in sequence is reading the changelog of British democracy.",
