  "body": "# Act of Settlement 1701\n\nThe statute that decides who wears the Crown — and, almost incidentally, the statute\nthat made judges independent of the Crown.\n\n## What it changed\n\n- **Succession settled by Parliament.** The Crown was settled on Electress Sophia of\n  Hanover and \"the heirs of her body being Protestants\". Monarchs must be in communion\n  with the Church of England. The message beneath the genealogy: the Crown is a\n  creature of statute, and Parliament can rewrite the succession.\n- **Judicial independence.** Judges' commissions were placed *quamdiu se bene\n  gesserint* — \"during good behaviour\" — with salaries \"ascertained and established\",\n  removable only on the address of both Houses of Parliament. Before this, judges\n  served at the King's pleasure and were dismissed for inconvenient rulings.\n- **Ministerial accountability.** A royal pardon cannot be pleaded to bar an\n  impeachment by the Commons.\n\n## Chamber record\n\nPassed by the Parliament of England with Royal Assent on 12 June 1701, the Act executed\nflawlessly thirteen years later: on Queen Anne's death in 1714 the Crown passed over\ndozens of Catholic claimants with superior hereditary titles to George I of Hanover.\nIts religious and gender provisions stood for three centuries until the Succession to\nthe Crown Act 2013 — agreed by all sixteen Commonwealth realms — amended them."
