  "body": "# Magna Carta\n\nThe Great Charter is the root inscription of the English constitution: the first\nacknowledgement in writing that the Crown itself is under the law.\n\n## What it changed\n\nThe 1215 Charter forced King John to concede that royal power had limits enforceable by\nhis subjects: no taxation without common counsel, no sale or denial of justice, no\nimprisonment of a free man except by lawful judgment of his equals or the law of the land.\n\n## The surviving text\n\nOnly three chapters of the 1297 statute remain in force in England and Wales:\n\n- **Chapter 1** — the English Church shall be free.\n- **Chapter 9** — the City of London shall retain its ancient liberties.\n- **Chapter 29** — *\"No freeman shall be taken or imprisoned … but by lawful judgment of\n  his peers, or by the law of the land. We will sell to no man, we will not deny or defer\n  to any man either justice or right.\"*\n\nChapter 29 (clauses 39 and 40 of the 1215 Charter) is the ancestor of due process\nguarantees worldwide, including the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments to the US\nConstitution.\n\n## Chamber record\n\nMagna Carta predates Parliament. It was extracted at sword-point, annulled by a pope,\nreissued three times, and finally confirmed as statute by Edward I in 1297 in exchange\nfor taxation — a reminder that constitutional history is a negotiation, not a ceremony."
